From f99e281e886e1ffa98dd9b167304ef123d7105f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emergent-agent-e1 <emergent-agent-e1@nivxray>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:31:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/13] File Trajectory: find a SHA-256 across one customer's
 endpoints
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Files was disabled with 'the sensor does not collect file-system
observation yet'. That was true of the substrate file_trajectory.py
reads — v2_shadow_observations.artefacts.file[].sha256 is empty — but
not of the sensor: every PROCESS_OBSERVED event carries the digest of
the executed image in edr_raw_events.payload.sha256.

Verified before writing a line of it, on tenant default's 360,660 raw
events: payload.sha256 is never the record's own payload_sha256 (0 of
1001 sampled), 55 distinct digests map to stable image paths, and one
digest appears under 14 paths — the same content run from /usr/bin/sleep
and from copies under /var/tmp. That is a file trajectory.

  GET /api/edr/files/{sha256}/sightings — customer resolved server-side
  by edr_tenant, so a hash is only ever a key within one customer.

A sighting is dated by payload.observed_at, never by ingest_time: five
sightings in this substrate were observed on one UTC day and delivered
on the next, and an analyst sent to the delivery day is sent to a day
the evidence is not on. Those rows are surfaced separately so a capped
read cannot hide them.

Clicking a sighting opens that endpoint's Device Trajectory in a new tab
at the observed instant, leaving the file investigation open. What this
is NOT is stated on the page: executed-image digests only, so silence
here is not proof a file is absent.
